8468017578125, 0.01152801513671875, -0.01352691650390625, 0.053466796875, -0.035430908203125, 0.003902435302734375, -0.01409149169921875, -0.0175628662109375, -0.0197906494140625, -0.01291656494140625, 0.05584716796875, 0.02191162109375, 0.0210418701171875, 0.0254364013671875, 0.01465606689453125, 0.002941131591796875, 0.00743865966796875, -0.0286865234375, 0.01177978515625, -0.00899505615234375, -0.00287628173828125, 0.0484619140625, -0.0270843505859375, 0.0136566162109375, -0.0019702911376953125, 0.0027256011962890625, 0.01345062255859375, 0.01522064208984375, 0.01161956787109375, -0.01371002197265625, 0.001377105712890625, 0.01715087890625, -0.007686614990234375, 0.0208740234375, -0.01074981689453125, 0.01146697998046875, -0.005130767822265625, 0.02349853515625, 0.01520538330078125, -0.00473785400390625, 0.0134735107421875, -0.008941650390625, -0.032562255859375, -0.01450347900390625, -0.01515960693359375, 0.0181121826171875, -0.03948974609375, 0.0244903564453125, 0.007091522216796875, 0.016632080078125, -0.00685882568359375, -0.01552581787109375, -0.01194000244140625, 0.0217437744140625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave, and how they are perceived by higher-level players, are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. These models distinguish between two related but distinct constructs, which we define and operationalize separately:\n\n**Definitions**\n\n- **Level-0 Actions (Behavioral):** These are the actual choices made by teams instructed not to engage in any explicit strategic reasoning about others\u2019 moves. Actions are the team\u2019s decisions, not influenced by what they expect others to do. Previous literature sometimes models level-0 actions as random (uniform over the available options; see Nagel, 1995), or based on salient features such as prominent numbers (Burchardi & Penczynski, 2014). In our operationalization, a salient feature is a property of an option that makes it stand out perceptually or structurally (e.g., \"midpoint,\" \"highest number,\" \"unique endpoint\") in the set of possible choices.\n- **Level-0 Beliefs (Expectational):** These refer to a team's stated expectations about what other, similarly non-strategic (level-0) teams would choose *in the same context*. These beliefs are elicited directly, require that the team forecast the aggregate behavior of other teams also acting non-strategically, and do not refer to beliefs about members within one\u2019s own team.\n\nWhile both constructs intend to capture non-strategic (\u201cna\u00efve\u201d) thought, they are conceptually and empirically distinct. Actions are team choices in the absence of iterative reasoning; beliefs are forecasts about others\u2019 likely level-0 actions under the same non-strategic constraints.\n\n_Examples that highlight coding boundaries_: Choosing \u201c50 because it is in the middle\u201d is coded as attention to a salient feature and considered level-0. Choosing \u201c20 because I think others will go lower\u201d involves iteration and is not level-0. Selecting \u201c20 because it is the highest possible, and higher is better\u201d is considered level-0 if there is no reference to other players. An ambiguous case is \u201c17, because it feels significant based on numbers I\u2019ve seen before\u201d\u2014which coders resolved as level-0 only if no belief about other teams\u2019 likely choices was expressed.\n\n**Operationalization**\n\nIn our study, **level-0 actions** are defined as the action selected by each team of two participants, after intra-team discussion but without reference to what others might do. **Level-0 beliefs** are separately elicited forecasts by each team about the distribution of choices they expect from other similarly non-strategic teams, again under instructions to disregard strategic considerations. Elicitation occurs in separate tasks to avoid cross-contamination. All measurements and coding occur at the team rather than the individual level.\n\n**Coding Protocol and Reliability**\n\n*Sample Description:* There were 25 teams total (50 subjects): 14 teams in the beauty contest, 11 teams in the 11-20 game. All teams were formed and assigned to games by random draw. Session order was randomized, and task presentation was counterbalanced to minimize order effects.\n\n*Coding Rubric:* Teams were classified as level-0 if their written justifications, coded by pairs of trained raters using an explicit rubric, met criteria adapted from Burchardi & Penczynski and Goeree et al. (2018), summarized below:\n\n| Criterion                              | Yes (Level-0) Example                                                 | No (Non-Level-0) Example                                            |\n|-----------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Absence of strategic/iterated reasoning | \u201cWe picked 50 because it\u2019s the center.\u201d                               | \u201cWe picked 33 because others will pick 50, so 2/3\u00d750=33.\u201d           |\n| Reliance on salient features            | \u201c20 is the largest possible number.\u201d                                  | \u201cWe expect others to pick 18, so we pick 17 to go lower.\u201d           |\n| Payoff structure without iteration      | \u201c20 is the safest and highest.\u201d (\u201cSafe\u201d: maximal without penalty)     | \u201cWe chose 18 to beat average strategies.\u201d                           |\n| No explicit probabilistic/statistical talk | \u201cThis number stands out.\u201d                                          | \u201cStatistically, the average will be near 17.\u201d                       |\n\nAmbiguous justifications\u2014e.g., \u201cPick 17, it just feels right\u201d\u2014were discussed by coders. When reasoning hinted at implicit forecasts about others, it was only coded level-0 if the justification included *no* reference (even indirect) to others\u2019 likely actions. Coders received standardized training and calibration using edge cases. Full rubric summary and coding clarifications are available in Appendix A.\n\n*Coder Reliability and Blinding:* Coders were blind to team identity and session order. In ambiguous cases, raters discussed until consensus, using protocol guidelines. Final percent agreement was 92%; Cohen\u2019s kappa = 0.88, indicating high inter-rater reliability.\n\n**Tables and Figures**\n\n- **Table 1** reports anonymized sample justifications by coding category. Panel A shows typical level-0 beauty contest reasoning (\u201c50 is in the middle and stands out\u201d); Panel B presents 11-20 game justifications (\u201c20 is the safest and highest; maximizing base payoff\u201d); Panel C includes rare cases of action-belief divergence (e.g., \u201cAction: safest choice for payoff; Belief: others may be more conservative\u201d).\n- **Figure 1:** Panel A displays histograms for team-level actions and beliefs in the beauty contest (responses cluster sharply at ~50); Panel B does the same for the 11-20 game (responses cluster near 20, avoiding minimum values); Panel C highlights outlier sessions where actions and beliefs diverged. Figures are based on team-level responses, not individuals.\n\n**Experimental Contexts**\n\n1. **Beauty Contest (Burchardi & Penczynski, 2014):** Teams choose a number from 0\u2013100; the winner is closest to two-thirds of the group average. Prior work finds level-0 actions often cluster at clear, salient values\u2014particularly the midpoint (50).\n2. **11-20 Game (Goeree et al., 2018):** Teams select an integer 11\u201320, earn that number in base payoff, and a bonus for being uniquely lowest. Level-0 behaviors often favor salient endpoints or payoff-salient numbers, not randomization or simple minimization.\n\n**Quantitative Results**\n\nIn the beauty contest, for the 14 teams classified as level-0:\n- **Mean action:** 51.2 (SD = 7.1, 95% CI [46.7, 55.7])\n- **Mean level-0 belief:** 49.8 (SD = 7.9, 95% CI [44.7, 54.9])\n- **Divergence:** Only one team deviated by more than 10 from the midpoint; most clustered at or near 50.\n- **Refer to Table 1, Panel A** for representative justifications.\n\nIn the 11-20 game, among 11 level-0 teams:\n- **Mean action:** 18.7 (SD = 1.6, 95% CI [17.8, 19.6]); modal response: 20 (5 teams); no team chose 11; remainder chose 17\u201319.\n- **Mean level-0 belief:** 19.1 (SD = 1.3, 95% CI [18.3, 19.9])\n- **Refer to Table 1, Panel B** for representative reasoning.\n\n**Action\u2013Belief Differences:** Across both games, divergence between action and belief was rare. In only 2 out of 25 sessions (8%) did action and belief differ by more than 6 units\u2014a difference chosen a priori as substantively meaningful based on the dispersion of team choices. Most action\u2013belief differences clustered within \u00b12 units. No statistically significant difference between actions and beliefs was found (paired t-test, t(24) = 0.67, p = 0.51; mean absolute action\u2013belief difference = 1.2, 95% CI [0.2, 2.2]; Cohen\u2019s d = 0.13). The sample\u2019s modest size (N = 25 teams) implies that minimum detectable effects for such rare divergences would require a much larger sample; current results should be interpreted as suggestive rather than definitive.\n\n**Sample Size and Statistical Power**\n\nOur analysis is underpowered to detect small or infrequent action\u2013belief divergences or to enable subgroup comparisons (e.g., by prior exposure or demographic subgroup). As such, all mean and effect size estimates should be seen as preliminary, and we do not claim generalizability beyond the two games and team-based sessions examined here.\n\n**Interpretation and Context**\n\nOur intra-team elicitation approach replicates and extends Burchardi & Penczynski (2014) and Goeree et al. (2018) by confirming that level-0 teams typically anchor on payoff-salient or \u201csafe\u201d values\u2014those that maximize base payoff, are endpoints or midpoints in the available range, or are perceptually focal\u2014rather than randomization or undefined central tendency. Results support critiques by Crawford et al. (2013) that context and payoff structure, not acontextual randomization, drive \u201cnon-strategic\u201d choices.\n\n**Methodological Considerations and Limitations**\n\n- **External Validity and Generalizability:** Intra-team (group) settings may foster convergence and suppress minority perspectives (Janis, 1982), potentially leading to overestimation of consensus and bias toward easily justified or prominent response options. Individual settings may exhibit more heterogeneity and more \u201cpartial\u201d strategic elements. Thus, we do not generalize findings about action-belief correspondence or the prominence of salient features to individual choice populations or larger field samples without further study.\n- **Potential Coding Limitations:**"}}